{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Decentralized network operators often have different threat models and budgets, whereas a single large service has a unified threat model and budget","In a large service, operational decisions can be made uniformly and deployed atomically; in a federation, updates propagate slowly and inconsistently","The supply chain of a federation is spread across many vendors and operators, whereas a monolith concentrates its dependency surface in one team"],"overturn_conditions":["Evidence that decentralized networks are systematically more resilient even under coordination attacks (link failures, DNS, split-brain) would overturn the claim that adaptive routing is the operative property","Evidence that large services cannot or do not adopt modular degradation paths at the same cost as small federations would overturn the claim that monoliths can recover the same property by choice","Evidence that the shared cause of most large-service failures is in the execution cores themselves, not in the shared governance layer, would weaken the argument that monoliths' weakness is their coupling"],"shared_structure":["Both systems are presented with the same trade-off: modular degradation vs. tightly coupled redundancy","In both, \"resilience\" is not a fixed property but an outcome that depends on the operator's prior decisions about fallback paths","In both, the choice is sensitive to the attack surface of the shared cause: if the shared cause attacks nodes, independent operation helps; if it attacks coordination links, concentrated operation helps"],"source_memory_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"],"transfer_claims":["If a large service operator deliberately adopts bulkheading, independent deployment of redundant components, and adversarial coordination testing, it will recover the modular benefits that small networks get by default, and in some cases will outperform a loose federation because it can also afford better coordination governance","If a decentralized network has never tested its fallback links under a realistic coordination attack, its apparent resilience is an artifact of sampling bias, not a robust property","Distinguishing evidence: post-incident reports of monolith failures should show whether the failure was in the execution cores (spared by redundancy) or in the shared deployment / governance layer (not spared)"]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Decentralized networks of small services exhibit apparent resilience only because their weaker individual reliability forces operators to rely on modular fallback; this contingency-derived modularity, not decentralization itself, is what breaks correlation. Under a shared-cause perturbation that spares fallback links but kills primary execution environments, large monoliths with N+1 redundant execution cores absorb the shock; under a perturbation that kills primary links, small networks survive only because their operators have already built adaptive detours. The robust property is adaptive routing topology, not service size.","counterconditions":["If adaptive routing alone were sufficient, networks that deliberately disable centralized coordination (no BGP, no authority) would be most resilient; they are not.","If weakness-induced modularity were the only source of resilience, then a single-service architecture whose operator deliberately chose modular degradation paths would match decentralized resilience — but it would not, because independence of operation also implies independence of failure observability and response","Evidence of catastrophic decentralized network failure during regional power grid collapse would weaken the claim"],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","quote":"school_policy"},{"block":"74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","quote":"school_policy"}],"mechanism":"Independently-operated services in a loose federation are usually individually less reliable. This unsparing selection pressure forces operators to build modular degradation paths, bulkheaded retry policies, and eventually consistent state recovery. In a large, redundant monolith, because all N+1 execution cores share a development team and a release pipeline, failures that are not independent (supply-chain CVEs, bad deployments) kill all cores at once; but because cores are physically separated and carefully mirrored, environmental perturbations are absorbed by hot spares. So the \"resilience\" of decentralized networks is not a gift of size but a side effect of the adaptive detours their weakness has already necessitated. When the shared cause attacks nodes directly (log4j-style RCE), decentralization helps by letting unaffected nodes keep routing. When the shared cause attacks links or coordination state (split-brain, DNS poisoning), the monolith's internal redundancy, protected by a single governance boundary, is more robust than a mesh whose adaptive protocols were never stress-tested under adversarial routing.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.47}],"simulation_proposals":[]}